@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Accordion}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Accordion @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Accordion @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>

<p>
    This Accordion component was built following the W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG). By adhering to standardised patterns, we ensure that the component 
    behaves predictably across different browsers and assistive technologies.
</p>
<p>
    Each AccordionItem has a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">PanelHasTabIndex</span> parameter, which by default is true. When true a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tabindex="0"</span> is added to the associated panel.
    If you know there will be no focusable elements in the panel or the panel is scrollable then you should leave this set to true.
    Without this, when the user is on a AccordionItem header (and the content is shown) pressing the Tab key they would go past the panel and onto the next AccordionItem or next focusable element on the page 
    and/or may not be able to scroll the panel content.
</p>
<p>
    If you know there will be a focusable element at the start of the panel content or there is a heading at the start of the panel and it's not scrollable,
    then you can set the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">PanelHasTabIndex</span> parameter to false to omit the tabindex on the Panel.
</p>
<p>
    If the first focusable element is in the middle of the panel, past important content, then I would retain the tabindex on the panel, so a screen reader user can read from the start of the panel content.
</p>
<h2 id="accordion-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="accordion-keyboard">
    <li>
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> - when focus moves into the accordion, places focus onto the the first or last accordion item heading / button.
        <br />When the accordion item is expanded focus moves to the first focusable element within the accordion panel or focus is set on the panel itself.
        <br />When the accordion item is collapsed focus moves to the next accordion heading or off the accordion going to the next focusable element on the page.
    </li>
    <li><kbd>Shift + Tab</kbd> reverses tab direction. See above.</li>
    <li><kbd>Up Arrow</kbd> - moves focus to the previous accordion heading. If the focus is on the first heading, moves focus to the last heading.</li>
    <li><kbd>Down Arrow</kbd> - moves focus to the next accordion heading. If the focus is on the last tab, moves focus to the first heading.</li>
    <li><kbd>Space or Enter</kbd> - expands or collapses the selected accordion item.</li>
    <li><kbd>Home</kbd> - moves focus to the first according heading.</li>
    <li><kbd>End</kbd> - moves focus to the last accordion heading.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>These keyboard interactions are primarily aimed at keyboard-only users. Screen reader users will often navigate differently using their assistive technology's 
    built-in commands, though the patterns also apply when a screen reader is operating in interaction mode.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>

<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator</b> when paired with Chrome or Edge, more often than not will not announce that the accordion item has been expanded after activation.
        However, it does so consistently when paired with Firefox.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>NVDA</b> on all browsers. When in Browse mode on clicking a collapsed accordion header it will announce that its expanded, twice.
        In Focus mode it works as expected.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>JAWS</b> paired with Firefox will sometimes repeat the accordion heading names and actions, expanded/collapsed.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>VoiceOver</b> on the macOS paired with Safari seems to stutter on expanding accordion items.
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> The same quirks were also noted on the APG example. 
</p>

